[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: A hand using a deburring tool on a 3D-printed part, removing excess material.
AUDIO: 'This video is all about lesser-known tips&nbsp; and tricks that I actually use myself daily,&nbsp;&nbsp; and I have no doubt they will change parts of&nbsp; your post-3D-printing routine too. So, without&nbsp;&nbsp; further ado, let’s see which things have proven&nbsp; themselves over time and are not just gimmicks. &nbsp; To start with, the thing I use most often&nbsp; after a 3D print finishes is this stand for&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:18] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Thermal camera view of a heated object, showing temperature readings.
AUDIO: 'hot flex plates. You should never remove the&nbsp; plate immediately when printing larger models,&nbsp;&nbsp; but when printing smaller PLA, PETG,&nbsp; or even ASA parts, I almost never wait&nbsp;&nbsp; for them to fully cool down inside the printer. You might ask what the point of such a stand is if&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we can just place the flex plate on anything&nbsp; that will withstand the heat from it. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp; unless you clean the surface before&nbsp; setting the plate down each time,&nbsp;&nbsp; it can attract all kinds of dirt and debris. With a stand like this printed from ASA, ABS,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:48] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A 3D-printed grid structure resting on a pile of sunflower seeds, with a meme reaction image in the corner.
AUDIO: 'or at least glass-fiber or carbon-fiber-filled&nbsp; PETG, you can simply take out the plate and&nbsp;&nbsp; place it on any surface without worrying&nbsp; about it. When I’m feeling extra YOLO,&nbsp;&nbsp; I even use an additional fan to speed up the&nbsp; cooldown, but only when the parts are very small. &nbsp;'

[01:03] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A white 3D-printed stand on a metallic build plate.
AUDIO: 'Basically, the bigger the part, the more&nbsp; time it should spend cooling down inside the&nbsp;&nbsp; printer before you remove it. This is because&nbsp; after printing is done, the bottom layers are&nbsp;&nbsp; still very hot and slightly soft, and forcefully&nbsp; bending the plate can permanently deform them. &nbsp;'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'However, even after the plate has fully cooled&nbsp; down, some prints, especially PETG, can still be&nbsp;&nbsp; difficult to remove. On top of that, the bottom&nbsp; surfaces often develop stress marks similar to&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:30] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A metal scraping tool (BIGTREETECH Panda Edge) removing residue from a textured build plate.
AUDIO: 'the ones you see when you bend filament. To solve&nbsp; these problems, there are some great solutions. &nbsp; The most annoying things to remove are&nbsp; those thin skirts and brims. Having a&nbsp;&nbsp; proper scraping tool completely changes how easy&nbsp; they are to remove. I used to print such tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but they are just no match. I also found&nbsp; that spraying some rubbing alcohol in those&nbsp;&nbsp; sections makes the process even easier. There are plenty of scraper options to&nbsp;&nbsp; choose from, and you can even print one&nbsp; yourself. All you need are the blades,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:00] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A hand peeling a thin, flexible sheet off a build plate.
AUDIO: 'so there is really no excuse not to have one.&nbsp; And if nothing else helps, print a two-layer&nbsp;&nbsp; sheet over the problematic area. It will pick up&nbsp; the filament leftovers embedded in the plate. &nbsp; When it comes to those stress marks on&nbsp; the bottom of prints, people often say a&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:16] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A small blowtorch being used to heat the surface of a black 3D-printed disc.
AUDIO: 'heat gun is the best way to get rid of them.&nbsp; But in my experience, the fastest and most&nbsp;&nbsp; convenient solution is a small blowtorch. The goal is to slightly melt only the outer&nbsp;&nbsp; surface of the print so it becomes shiny while&nbsp; spending as little time as possible, for obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reasons. It is almost instant, gives great&nbsp; results, but does require some experience, as&nbsp;&nbsp; using the wrong power setting, distance, or moving&nbsp; too slowly can ruin the print very quickly. &nbsp; Plus, it is an actual blowtorch that can set&nbsp; things on fire, so don’t be careless with it.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:48] [scene:result]
VISUAL: Close-up of a 3D-printed part with stringing, before treatment.
AUDIO: 'If you know what you are doing, it is amazing&nbsp; not only for fixing those surfaces but also&nbsp;&nbsp; for instantly removing stringing from prints. Just&nbsp; don’t buy a blowtorch that isn’t powerful enough.&nbsp;&nbsp; It makes the process far too slow. The most&nbsp; important thing is that it would have plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:05] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Hands assembling a circular metal component with screws.
AUDIO: 'of adjustable power and feel comfortable&nbsp; in your hand, with a quick on/off switch. &nbsp; Speaking of comfort and control, what if&nbsp; you don’t have any? During this video,&nbsp;&nbsp; I was designing and making probably the&nbsp; silliest thing I have ever built. The tip&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here for makers is that plastics can withstand&nbsp; quite a lot of force when it’s secured to&nbsp;&nbsp; metal parts with a large enough contact area. This is where today’s video sponsor, PCBWAY, comes&nbsp;&nbsp; in. They manufactured this beautiful anodized&nbsp; aluminum adapter to do exactly that – provide&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:35] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A person playing a racing game with a custom 3D-printed steering wheel.
AUDIO: 'more surface area for mounting this plastic&nbsp; ship wheel printed from the updated Bambu PETG. &nbsp; I will be experimenting with a lot of silly&nbsp; things to mount, so having metal threads for&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'repeatedly screwing in different designs&nbsp; will greatly improve the durability. So,&nbsp;&nbsp; if you need all sorts of professional advanced&nbsp; manufacturing capabilities for your projects,&nbsp;&nbsp; be sure to check out PCBWay using&nbsp; the link in the video description. &nbsp;'

[04:01] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A 3D printer in operation, printing a black object on a build plate labeled "Powered by ENERGETIC 3D".
AUDIO: 'When it comes to making all sorts of things,&nbsp; sometimes you simply have to use a brim to make&nbsp;&nbsp; sure the part warps as little as possible&nbsp; and stays securely attached to the build&nbsp;&nbsp; plate. I already explained in detail why warping&nbsp; happens in the first place and how to reduce it,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but if you use brims, a deburring tool is a&nbsp; must-have. Removing brims with this thing is an&nbsp;&nbsp; absolute breeze. It is night and day compared to&nbsp; using a regular knife, and it is much safer too. &nbsp;'

[04:27] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A soldering iron with a special tip inserting a brass heat insert into a 3D-printed part.
AUDIO: 'However, one of the best hacks, and&nbsp; the one I’ve been using the longest,&nbsp;&nbsp; has to be this trick for installing very&nbsp; commonly used heat inserts. All you need&nbsp;&nbsp; is some brass tips for your soldering iron. Heat&nbsp; it up, press the insert in, but not all the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:42] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A person holding a 3D-printed filament respooler adapter next to a DeWalt drill.
AUDIO: 'and then use almost any flat metal object to press&nbsp; it perfectly flush. It works wonders every time,&nbsp;&nbsp; and there’s no need for a dedicated heat insert&nbsp; station. It is simple yet incredibly effective. &nbsp;'

[04:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'When it comes to filament management, a universal&nbsp; drill adapter for quickly respooling filament&nbsp;&nbsp; is something I use far more often than I ever&nbsp; expected. Got a filament with an unconventional&nbsp;&nbsp; spool that doesn’t fit where you need it?&nbsp; Just place the original spool on anything that&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:12] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A person respooling filament from one spool to another using a drill and the adapter.
AUDIO: 'lets it spin freely without too much friction.&nbsp; Attach an empty spool to the drill adapter,&nbsp;&nbsp; feed the filament through a PTFE tube as a&nbsp; guide, and let the drill do the hard work. &nbsp; It takes some practice, but it’s by far the&nbsp; fastest way to respool filament and be done&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with it. I often use it when I want to combine&nbsp; a few partially used spools into one. That&nbsp;&nbsp; also requires filament fusing, which, I’ll be&nbsp; honest, is a bit of a janky process and almost&nbsp;&nbsp; always takes a few tries. Because of that, I&nbsp; mainly do it with two spools that still have&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a decent amount of filament left. It’s a great&nbsp; way to organize and clean up your workspace. &nbsp; When it comes to storing filament, a good&nbsp; spool design makes life easy. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp; a poor design is annoying, as it not only wastes&nbsp; filament but also needs cutting off the bent&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:58] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A container filled with small, dark 3D-printed filament clips.
AUDIO: 'end each time you use it. In those cases, I just&nbsp; clamp the ends of the filament with these simple&nbsp;&nbsp; filament clips. There are countless designs&nbsp; available, but after trying different ones,&nbsp;&nbsp; I always end up printing more of these.&nbsp; They are just simple yet very effective. &nbsp;'

[06:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you care a lot about print quality, you’re&nbsp; probably trying to keep your filament as dry as&nbsp;&nbsp; possible. That usually means printing containers&nbsp; for desiccant and storing them together with&nbsp;&nbsp; your filament. The annoying part is sealing&nbsp; the bags and putting them back into the box,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'especially with those aluminum foil bags.&nbsp; They just don’t want to go back in. &nbsp; Quite recently I had an idea: why not print an&nbsp; adapter for these inexpensive yet surprisingly&nbsp;&nbsp; powerful blowers that would suck air out instead?&nbsp; Well, it worked even better than I expected,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:45] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A person holding a Wolfbox MF50 mini blower, pointing to its nozzle.
AUDIO: 'and it has become part of my filament storage&nbsp; routine. Keep in mind that none of these bags&nbsp;&nbsp; have a perfect seal, so they’ll slowly let air&nbsp; back in over time. That’s why I put them back&nbsp;&nbsp; into the box, as it helps limit that expansion. However, for precious engineering filaments such&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as PC, PET, PA, PPS, or PPA, I use double&nbsp; protection: desiccant containers inside&nbsp;&nbsp; aluminum foil bags, plus extra desiccant inside&nbsp; a large sealed box. This one from IKEA is very&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:14] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A clear plastic IKEA Samla box filled with individually bagged filament spools and desiccant containers.
AUDIO: 'popular and holds four spools. All you need&nbsp; to do is seal the lid with window sealing&nbsp;&nbsp; tape and print several clamps. This keeps your&nbsp; pricey filaments dry for much, much longer. &nbsp;'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, these are my genuine tips and tricks&nbsp; that I actually use on a regular basis,&nbsp;&nbsp; not just clickbaity things that often are&nbsp; just gimmicks. These things work great for me,&nbsp;&nbsp; so hopefully some of them will be useful to you&nbsp; as well. If you have any great tricks of your own,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:42] [scene:outro]
VISUAL: A screen displaying "CURRENTLY ACTIVE SUPPORTERS ON PATREON" and "CURRENTLY ACTIVE MEMBERS ON YOUTUBE" with lists of names.
AUDIO: 'make sure to share them in the comments. I&nbsp; always love hearing what actually works well. &nbsp; Huge thanks to PCBWAY for sponsoring this&nbsp; video, and to everyone who supports my&nbsp;&nbsp; work on YouTube and Patreon. Well, that’s&nbsp; it from me, and I’ll see you next time.'
